cartridge "BUMP 'N RUN/Tim J. Midkiff/1986"

' BUMP 'N RUN
' A ColecoVision game based on "Bump 'n Run", COMPUTE!'s Gazette disk of
' November 1986 (Commodore 64 machine language at $C000, started by a
' four-line BASIC boot; the program carries no author's name). Made for
' Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy
' Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's, read from its
' machine code (orig/dis.txt) and watched in a C64 emulator; the game is
' rebuilt for the ColecoVision: a bumper-car rink with padded rails and
' goal nets, two round bumper cars as smooth sprites, a ball, sound.
'
' The game: two bumper cars, PURPLE and YELLOW, and one ball. Drive over
' the free ball to take it, press the button to shoot it: it leaves with
' your car's speed and direction, and your car stops dead. The ball never
' slows down; it bounces off the rails until someone takes it. Bump the
' other car to swap speeds with it, and if either of you holds the ball,
' it changes hands. Shoot the ball into the far net (PURPLE scores on the
' right, YELLOW on the left); 15 goals win.
'
' From the original (orig/dis.txt): every moving thing has a speed per
' axis from -82 to 82 and a counter that starts at 90 and counts down by
' one each pass of the main loop; when it reaches the speed's size the
' thing moves one pixel (so a pass every 91 - |speed| passes), and a car
' then takes its stick: towards the pushed side 1 (2 if it was going the
' other way), nothing pushed slows it by 1. A car bounces off the rails
' (Y 79-210, X 44-301 in the C64's sprite pixels), the ball off Y 72-217
' and X 37-308 except in the goal mouths (ball Y 123-165), and the goal
' counts at X < 32 (YELLOW) or X >= 312 (PURPLE). The first pass of a bump
' swaps the two cars' speeds and the ball's holder; while the cars touch
' they are pushed one pixel apart each frame on each axis. The ball's
' shooter cannot take it back until it bounces once. 15 goals end the
' game. Start places: PURPLE X 74, YELLOW X 271, ball X 172, all at Y 144.
'
' Liberties: the C64 ran its loop about 24 times a frame (measured in
' jsc64: the cars' top speed was 3 pixels a frame); here 20 passes a
' frame (top speed 2.2 C64 pixels a frame) so a human can steer; the rink
' is drawn 49/64 of the C64's size while every rule stays in C64 pixels;
' round cars and touch tests by distance (the C64 used crosshair sprites
' and its collision register); the button must be pressed again to shoot
' after taking the ball (the C64 shot at once if the button was held: its
' latch at $C71B was never set); the ball leaves at once when shot and
' stays inside its holder (the C64 kept moving the hidden ball); bumps
' cannot push a car through a rail; a computer driver for YELLOW in the
' 1 PLAYER game (the C64 needed two joysticks); sounds, a title tune and a
' squash when cars bump (the C64 was silent); a title page and credits;
' "GAME OVER" then the title (the C64 said PRESS FIRE TO RESTART).
'
' Controls: controller 1 drives PURPLE, controller 2 YELLOW (1 PLAYER:
' either controller drives PURPLE). The stick accelerates, a button
' shoots. On the title, up and down choose (1 PLAYER, 2 PLAYERS, HOW TO
' PLAY, CREDITS), a button chooses; HOW TO PLAY has two pages (a button
' turns the page, 30 s idle goes back).
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

const TFl0 = 128
const TFl1 = 129
const TFl2 = 130
const TClL = 131
const TClR = 132
const THw = 133
const TVw = 134
const TCnr = 135
const TPost = 136
const TNet = 137
const TIcp = 138
const TIcy = 139
const TTb = 140
const TTm = 141
const SCar = 0
const SCarsq = 4
const SBall = 8
const NTiles8 = 112
const SprBytes = 96
const SndTuneA = 1
const SndTuneB = 2
const SndBump = 3
const SndWall = 4
const SndCatch = 5
const SndShoot = 6
const SndGoal = 7
const SndStart = 8
const SndOver = 9
const NSpr = 4
const NIter = 20
const Period = 90
const VMax = 82
const CpuMax = 40

' a car or the ball: X (9 bits) and Y in the C64's sprite pixels
record Obj:
  u16 X
  u8 Y
end record

